.TH VAULT-ABSORB 1 "October 2026" "vault" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vault-absorb \- add the files in a tar archive to an encrypted vault
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vault absorb
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-vault
.IR DIR ]
.RI [ FOLDER ]
.B <
.I ARCHIVE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vault absorb
reads a tar archive from standard input and stores every regular file in
it, beneath
.I FOLDER
if one is given, keeping the paths, permissions and modification times
recorded in the archive. A gzip-compressed archive is detected and
unpacked automatically.
.PP
Each file is encrypted straight from the stream; nothing is unpacked to
disk. Anything already stored under the same name is replaced.
.PP
Directory entries are not stored (directories exist implicitly). Symbolic
and hard links, devices and other special entries are reported on standard
error and skipped, as is any entry whose name would escape
.I FOLDER
through
.BR .. .
A leading
.B ./
or
.B /
is dropped.
.PP
The passphrase prompt reads from the terminal, not standard input, so
it still works with an archive piped in. It waits until the archive starts
arriving, so a program feeding the pipe, such as
.BR gpg (1)
asking for its own passphrase, has finished with the terminal first.
An empty pipe fails without asking at all.
The vault is locked exclusively for the whole run.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-key " PATH"
Key file. Default: $VAULT_KEY, else ~/.vault/key.
.TP
.BI \-\-vault " DIR"
Vault directory. Default: $VAULT_DIR, else ~/.vault/store.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VAULT_PASSPHRASE
Passphrase, instead of prompting on the terminal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if at least one file was stored and the archive was read to the end.
1 if the archive is malformed or ends early, holds no regular files, or
anything fails; files stored before the failure stay in the vault.
.SH EXAMPLES
Unpack an existing archive into the vault under
.BR backup/ :
.PP
.RS
vault absorb backup < photos.tar.gz
.RE
.PP
Store a directory under
.BR backup/photos/ :
.PP
.RS
tar \-cz photos | vault absorb backup
.RE
.PP
Pull a remote directory straight into the vault:
.PP
.RS
ssh server 'tar \-C /etc \-c .' | vault absorb server1/etc
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vault (1),
.BR vault\-add (1),
.BR tar (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Andy Dixon <vault@dixon.cx>, https://www.dixon.cx
